<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mengujekids</h1><p class="st-meta">1072 CE – 1277 CE (approx.) · capital Erzincan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish dynasty of the upper Euphrates whose masterpiece, the Great Mosque and Hospital of Divrigi, is a World Heritage Site.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengujek-gazi">Mengujek Gazi</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemah">Kemah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divrigi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebinkarahisar">Sebinkarahisar</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, establishing a small principality between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saltukids">Saltukids</a> and the Byzantine and Georgian frontiers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mengujekids were patrons of Persian culture and architecture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-bahramshah">Fakhr al-Din Bahramshah of Erzincan</a>, who ruled for some sixty years, received the dedication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami's</a> poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhzan-al-asrar">Makhzan al-Asrar</a>. In 1228-1229 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-of-divrigi">Ahmad Shah of Divrigi</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turan-malik">Turan Malik</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi-great-mosque-and-hospital">Great Mosque and Hospital of Divrigi</a>, whose carved stone portals are among the most extraordinary in medieval Islamic art. Their towns had large Armenian and Greek populations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> absorbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> branch around 1228 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divrigi</a> branch survived as Seljuk and then Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until it disappears from the record in 1277.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mengujekids.jpg" alt="Mengujekids" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_Meng%C3%BCjek_(territories).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1072 CE – 1277 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erzincan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mengujek Gazi, Fakhr al-Din Bahramshah, Ahmad Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mengujekid dynasty, Mengücekoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=mengujekids" aria-label="Mengujekids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5654 755 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mengujekids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5974 1011L5810 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5974 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5974 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Erzincan. <a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=mengujekids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

